many people believe that central bankers  are asleep at the wheel and they're not  paying attention to bitcoin or the  lightning network but we now have  verifiable proof that that is not the  case the federal reserve bank of  cleveland recently released a white  paper analyzing the lightning network  and talking about how it will help  bitcoin transition into a better means  of payment that's not exactly what  you're hearing in the mainstream media  the paper was published in june of 2022  and as gabor points out here the federal  reserve bank of cleveland wrote that  paper of how it's turning bitcoin into  money bitcoin innovation is tangible  real and increasingly recognized haters  are in disbelief  as we look at this paper we can see the  lightning network turning bitcoin into  money it's a means of netting bitcoin  payments outside of blockchain and they  find a significant association between  lightning adoption and reduced  blockchain congestion now if we jump  into the piece itself there's three  specific parts that i think are worth  calling out the first is that they  outline four specific conclusions they  believe that the development of the  lightning network may have consequences  for welfare first bitcoin has become a  more efficient payment system making  users better off  second fewer transactions needed to be  recorded on layer one means that less  memory and energy are needed to run a  bitcoin node  third by reducing fees the lightning  network reduces the incentive for  bitcoin miners to use large amounts of  computing power meaning less energy use  and positive consequences for the  environment and fourth less blockchain  congestion may mean lower barriers to  arbitrage across cryptocurrency exchange  thereby improving market liquidity as  well now the federal reserve bank of  cleveland also finds that adoption of  the lightning network has led to a  reduction in bitcoin blockchain  congestion and lower mining fees the  results they're significant and they  cannot be explained by any other changes  including in demand for blockchain space  nor by other technological developments  they believe that lightning network can  help bitcoin achieve greater scalability  allowing it to operate better as a  payment system according to their  results if the lightning network had  existed in 2017 congestion could have  been 93  lower the lightning network works in  their opinion now the federal reserve  bank of cleveland concludes that the  usage of the lightning network is  associated with reduced mempool  congestion in bitcoin and with those  lower fees they suggest that the  off-chain netting benefits of the  lightning network can help bitcoin to  scale and function better as a means of  payment they also believe that lightning  network loosens a key technological  constraint by allowing payments to be  settled more quickly  now you can take a couple of things away  from this white paper first the central  bankers are not asleep at the wheel  they're paying attention they're  studying this technology they're trying  to understand what works and what  doesn't work and to see them publish  something on the lightning network is  quite bullish in my opinion the second  thing is that even the central bankers  agree the lightning network that layer 2  scalability solution is making bitcoin a  better means of payment and if we can  reduce that technological barrier and  make bitcoin faster and cheaper to  transact it means that bitcoin has more  utility and is likely to be adopted by  more people so as a reminder no one  knows what the future holds but if the  central bankers are publishing white  papers today and they're actually being  bullish and positive on bitcoin and the  lightning network it gives me hope that  eventually we are going to see central  banks both in the united states and  globally start to adopt bitcoin and the  bitcoin payment rails it's simply  superior technology and at the end of  every single technology innovation what  we get is a winner based on what the  consumer wants and right now even the  central banks understand if this is  cheaper and faster eventually consumers  will adopt it
 

